 # Sphere Tracing with SDFs
 
-Also called: Sphere Marching, Ray Marching with SDFs
+Also called: Signed Distance Field Ray Marching, SDF Raymarching
 
 ## Overview
-Sphere Tracing is an image-order rendering algorithm for implicit surfaces defined by Signed Distance Functions (SDFs). It solves the problem of finding the intersection between a camera ray and complex, continuous geometry without requiring explicit polygonal meshes or bounding volume hierarchies. It sets itself apart from its sibling [Ray-marching on 3D Gaussians](1036-ray-marching-on-3d-gaussians.md) by using exact geometric distance bounds rather than probabilistic density accumulation, and from [Terrain Raymarching](1357-terrain-raymarching.md) by supporting arbitrary 3D topology rather than height-field constraints. It builds on [Distance-field ray marching](21-distance-field-ray-marching.md).
+Sphere Tracing with SDFs is a geometric ray-marching algorithm that renders implicit surfaces defined by Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) by iteratively advancing a ray along its direction by the distance value returned by the SDF at the current position [S1, S2, S5, S6, S12, unofficial]. It solves the problem of finding the intersection between a ray and complex, continuous geometry without requiring explicit polygonal meshes or voxel grids [S1, unofficial][S2]. What sets it apart from its siblings and nearest alternatives is its use of the SDF's mathematical property to guarantee a "safe" step size: because no geometry exists closer than $f(\mathbf{p})$ to the query point $\mathbf{p}$, the ray cannot overshoot or penetrate a surface, allowing for efficient convergence with a minimal number of iterations [S8, S27, S49, unofficial]. Unlike [FBM Sphere Tracing with BVH Acceleration](1728-fbm-sphere-tracing-with-bvh-acceleration.md) which accelerates noise evaluation, or [Coarse-to-Fine Sphere Tracing](4615-coarse-to-fine-sphere-tracing.md) which uses mipmaps, standard Sphere Tracing relies solely on the local distance field for stepping [S5, unofficial].
 
